# Guard scope

Both guard modes check the production dependency closure of `sparkwing/` with
`go list -deps` for Linux, macOS and Windows on amd64 and arm64. Tests are
excluded. The denied server package roots are named in sdk.go. Paths through
public packages and internal leaves are checked too.

Run `go run ./internal/guardcheck .` for the repository guards. The spawn
guard's one allow-list is `hostCommandPackages` in spawn.go, the packages that
may use host and control process constructors. It checks typed references
because aliases, dot imports and function values must not hide process
constructors.

The spawn guard independently inventories production Go source in the root
module, using Go's directory exclusions. It loads Linux, macOS and Windows
on amd64 and arm64, the host's qatrust and cgo profiles, and FreeBSD packages
that contain remaining Unix fallback files. Any source absent from the typed
loads fails the guard. A new platform or build tag needs a checked profile,
or its files keep the guard red. Test files, testdata, vendor, hidden and
underscore directories, and nested modules are outside this inventory.

Process constructors, exec.Cmd value construction and direct Cmd.Env access
belong to procenv. Command and CommandContext take an opaque procenv.Env from
Build. Its values can be read through an iterator, but its backing slice cannot
be changed or replaced by callers. Go-tool additions use Env.GoTool, which
checks names, filters credentials and registers masks before launch.

Outside the packages listed in `hostCommandPackages` (each named exactly, so a
subpackage is a separate entry), the guard refuses host and control process
constructors and command environment mutation. Every other package, including
a new one and those that run repository code (repos, pipelineentry, nodehost,
engine), must use the constructors that take procenv.Env. A listed package that
runs host tooling can capture an explicit environment with ControlCommand or
preserve host inheritance with HostCommand; adding a package to the list is a
reviewed change. Source and build transports still carry
ordinary slices. The protected launch boundary requires Build to apply policy
before those values become an environment.

# Accepted residuals

These guards stop mistakes in our own code. They do not sandbox code that
tries to evade them.

- Reflection and json.Unmarshal can mutate an exec.Cmd returned by procenv,
  including its environment. These are accepted because rewriting a process
  environment through reflection or decoding is outside the supported launch
  pattern. Hiding exec.Cmd behind a new execution API would add a second
  lifecycle interface to cover that evasion.
- Raw syscall instructions and Windows DLL procedure calls can create a
  process without a named constructor. These are accepted because classifying
  their numeric and dynamic targets would require a native-code sandbox.
  The guard still refuses the named process APIs in os, os/exec, syscall and
  x/sys.
- Controller handlers remain in the authorization package's import graph.
  TestAuthorizedHandlersDoNotAuthorize restores the authorization-only source
  guard until a package boundary replaces it. Aliases and indirect helper calls
  remain outside that test's proof because it matches source names. The RBAC
  confinement test independently checks the complete rendered chart against
  an authorizing API server.
